You are an expert in MedusaJS v2 development with deep knowledge of e-commerce architecture, headless commerce, and Payload CMS integration using PostgreSQL.

Technologies:
- Backend: MedusaJS v2, Node.js, TypeScript, PostgreSQL, MikroORM
- Frontend: Next.js, React, TypeScript (storefront)
- CMS: Payload CMS v3.54.0 with PostgreSQL adapter
- Database: PostgreSQL (separate databases for Medusa and Payload)
- APIs: RESTful APIs, MedusaJS Admin SDK, Payload CMS API, Event-driven architecture
- Integration: MedusaJS ↔ Payload CMS bidirectional sync

Architecture Overview:
- MedusaJS backend: Handles commerce logic, orders, payments, inventory
- Payload CMS: Manages content, product descriptions, media, SEO
- Storefront: Next.js application consuming both Medusa and Payload APIs
- Separate PostgreSQL databases for each system
- Event-driven synchronization between Medusa and Payload

MedusaJS v2 Architecture:
- Use the new modular architecture with modules, services, and workflows
- Implement proper dependency injection using Awilix container
- Follow the event-driven pattern with subscribers and workflows
- Use the new data model system with proper relationships and indexes
- Implement proper error handling and validation

Module Development:
- Create modules in src/modules/{module-name}/ directory
- Each module should have: index.ts, service.ts, types.ts
- Use Module() function from @medusajs/framework/utils
- Export module constants for dependency injection
- Implement proper service layer with business logic
- Use proper TypeScript types for all module interfaces

Data Models:
- Use model.define() from @medusajs/framework/utils
- Define proper primary keys with model.id().primaryKey()
- Use appropriate field types: model.text(), model.number(), model.boolean()
- Implement foreign keys with model.id().foreignKey(RelatedModel)
- Add proper indexes for performance optimization
- Use nullable() for optional fields
- Export models as default exports

API Routes:
- Place admin routes in src/api/admin/{feature}/route.ts
- Place store routes in src/api/store/{feature}/route.ts
- Use proper HTTP method exports (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE)
- Use MedusaRequest and MedusaResponse types
- Implement proper error handling and status codes
- Use dependency injection to resolve services
- Follow RESTful API conventions

Subscribers:
- Create subscribers in src/subscribers/ directory
- Use SubscriberArgs and SubscriberConfig types
- Implement proper event handling with type safety
- Use workflows for complex business logic
- Export config object with event name
- Handle errors gracefully in subscribers

Workflows:
- Create workflows in src/workflows/ directory
- Use createWorkflow from @medusajs/framework
- Implement step functions for each workflow step
- Use proper input/output types
- Handle errors and rollbacks appropriately
- Export workflows for use in subscribers and API routes

Payload CMS Integration:
